{"type": "vertex_text", "round": 3, "text": "I have completed the source-fidelity preflight review. The process involved a comprehensive comparison of the draft patent application materials against the authoritative inventor sources.\n\nFirst, I read and established the content of the inventor sources: `input/disclosure.md` and the USER-authored passages within `input/conversation.md`. These files were treated as the sole authority for the technical substance of the invention. I also read `input/brief.md` for filing formalities.\n\nNext, I read all draft application files, including `draft/09-claims.md` (claims), `draft/08-detailed-description.md` (detailed description), `draft/numerals.md` (list of numbered parts), `draft/07-drawings.md` (drawing descriptions), and all figure briefs located in the `figures/` directory.\n\nI then performed a full source ledgering. Every limitation in every claim was traced to an affirmative statement in the inventor sources. Every numbered part and its described structure and function in the detailed description and figure briefs was similarly traced. This verification extended to all specific relationships, materials, operating conditions, and optional embodiments presented in the draft.\n\nThe review also included a check for internal consistency. I verified that terminology was used consistently between the claims and the description, that each reference numeral corresponded to a single, consistently defined part, and that the figure descriptions and briefs were aligned with each other and with the text. No unresolved drafting notes, placeholders, or internal comments were found.\n\nThe analysis confirmed that all technical details in the claims and description are supported by the inventor's disclosure. The figure briefs, while providing specific guidance for schematic illustration, do not introduce new or unsupported technical substance but rather reflect reasonable depiction conventions for the disclosed structures and relationships. The entire application package was found to be internally consistent and faithful to the provided source materials."}
